# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "Reading from arrays", item 2.
id: array-read
title: Reading two neighbouring cells
topic: Arrays
level: 2
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "Reading from arrays" 2
tags: arrays, array read, definedness

task:
  Calculate the weakest precondition of the statement for the
  postcondition $Q$ with the rule ARRAY-READ. Unfold the definedness
  conditions and show that they can be written as the single condition
  $0 <= i && i + 1 < sizeof(a)$. Then use the consequence rule to decide
  whether the triple is valid.

pre: a[i] >= a[i + 1] && 0 <= i && i + 1 < sizeof(a)
post: x >= 0

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  x := a[i] - a[i + 1]     // i1
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of x := a[i] - a[i + 1]
rule: ARRAY-READ, WP
focus: i1
text:
  As for an assignment, we substitute the assigned variable ($x$) with the
  assignment expression ($a[i] - a[i + 1]$) in the postcondition $Q$. The
  expression reads the array twice, so ARRAY-READ adds one definedness
  condition per read: $def(a[i])$ and $def(a[i + 1])$.
calc:
  wp(x := a[i] - a[i + 1], Q)
  = (x >= 0)[x <- a[i] - a[i + 1]] && def(a[i]) && def(a[i + 1])
  = a[i] - a[i + 1] >= 0 && def(a[i]) && def(a[i + 1])     // x is replaced by a[i] - a[i + 1]
  = a[i] >= a[i + 1] && def(a[i]) && def(a[i + 1])         // add a[i + 1] to both sides
  = a[i] >= a[i + 1] && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) && 0 <= i + 1 < sizeof(a)     // def(a[J]) is 0 <= J < sizeof(a)
  = a[i] >= a[i + 1] && 0 <= i && i + 1 < sizeof(a)        // 0 <= i gives 0 <= i + 1, and i + 1 < sizeof(a) gives i < sizeof(a)
set: R1 = a[i] >= a[i + 1] && 0 <= i && i + 1 < sizeof(a)

step: P implies R1
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R1
text:
  $R1$ is the weakest precondition of the statement for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R1$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R1»
vc: a[i] >= a[i + 1] && 0 <= i && i + 1 < sizeof(a) ==> a[i] >= a[i + 1] && 0 <= i && i + 1 < sizeof(a)
why:
  $P$ and $R1$ are the same formula.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  Both reads, $a[i]$ and $a[i + 1]$, must be inside the array. The larger
  index $i + 1$ gives the upper bound, and the smaller index $i$ gives the
  lower bound.
